{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: close the replacement-object gap on the merge side of the boundary. I found this while reviewing your git-replacement-boundary head 7754607e, approved as dd57d71c, and I said in that verdict I would file it rather than leave it living only in review text.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT OPEN. 9e337057 asked for the immutable sequence trust boundary, and 7754607e closes it properly: every object-touching invocation in internal/gitstore now routes through storeGitArguments, mutation-pinned at both the helper and the call sites. That request named genesis, events, trees, payloads and checkpoints — all workroom-log objects — so the merge gate was correctly outside it. This request is the merge gate.\n\nTHE DEFECT. cmd/gs has its own git helper at cmd/gs/main.go:1884-1886. It builds [\"-C\", repo, ...] and passes no --no-replace-objects, and every merge-gate invocation goes through it. The one that matters is cmd/gs/main.go:524: git merge --no-ff --no-commit -- \u003ccandidate\u003e. So a refs/replace entry for the approved candidate, planted in the checkout repository, makes the merge take content other than the object the approval names — while the sealed receipt still records the approved OID. The ancestry test at :490, the HEAD reads at :460 and :485, and canonicalCommit at :963 have the same exposure.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. docs/concepts/work-loop.md states the property this breaks: merge \"hands git the approved object ID, never a branch name, so advancing the reviewed branch cannot retarget the merge\". A replacement ref retargets it anyway, through a channel that sentence does not consider. The log would then assert that a reviewed head landed when different content did, and every later reader would believe it, because the receipt is signed and internally consistent. That is worse than an ordinary bug: it is the log telling a lie it cannot detect.\n\nTHE HONEST BOUND ON SEVERITY, so nobody over-rates this. Planting refs/replace in the checkout requires write access to that repository, which under the trusted-process posture means the attacker is already inside the boundary SECURITY.md says is undefended. This is defence in depth and a correctness guarantee about our own tooling, not a fix for a remote attack. It is worth doing because the guarantee is cheap and the property is one we state in documentation.\n\nTHE SHAPE. Do what 7754607e did: fix the helper, not the call sites. That head is the reference implementation and it is approved — read storeGitArguments at internal/gitstore/gitstore.go and copy the shape, including the placement detail that --no-replace-objects must come before the subcommand or it is silently inert. Condition 3 is the one I care about most: a test that plants a real replacement ref and proves the merge ignores it, and that goes red when the guard is removed. Without that this is a habit rather than a guarantee, and the next helper will forget again.\n\nCondition 5 exists because I have not audited every cmd/gs invocation for whether some legitimately wants replacement semantics. I do not believe any does, but I would rather you tell me than assume I checked.","body":{"conditions":"1. Every git invocation cmd/gs makes that resolves, compares or merges a commit ignores replacement refs, including the merge itself at cmd/gs/main.go:524, the ancestry test at :490, the HEAD reads at :460 and :485, and canonicalCommit at :963. 2. The fix is at the shared helper (cmd/gs/main.go:1884), not sprinkled at call sites, matching the storeGitArguments shape approved at 7754607e. 3. A regression plants a refs/replace entry for an approved candidate in the checkout repository and proves gs merge lands the object the approval names rather than the replacement; it must fail when the guard is removed. 4. Invocations that resolve no object need not change, and the report says which were deliberately left. 5. If any invocation must honour replacement refs for a legitimate reason, say so explicitly rather than converting it silently. 6. make vet, make test, make race, make build, gofmt and diff-check pass at the exact head; artifact at cmd/gs; independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review before merge.","priority":"P2","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123","verified_at":"main 0ec3618f on 2026-08-21"}}